<title>INDIA: Indian political parties' positions in parliament.</title>
<headline>Indian political parties' positions in parliament.</headline>
<dateline>NEW DELHI 1997-04-02</dateline>
<text>
<p>Following are political parties' current positions in India's lower house of parliament, the Lok Sabha, which is scheduled to hold a vote of confidence in Prime Minister H.D. Deve Gowda on April 11.</p>
<p>Deve Gowda, whose coalition controls 180 votes, would need a simple majority of lawmakers present and voting to win the trust vote.</p>
<p>The 545-seat Lok Sabha currently has 542 members. Two seats are vacant and one is occupied by the speaker, P.A. Sangma, who can vote in the event of a tie.</p>
<p>The National Conference, a regional party from the northern state of Jammu and Kashmir, has no Lok Sabha members but is represented in the upper house, the Rajya Sabha, and is part of Deve Gowda's 15-party United Front coalition. -------------------------------------------------------------</p>
